{"version":1,"vintage":{"roads":"Natural Earth v5.1.2 (1:10m)","rail":"Natural Earth v5.1.2 (1:10m)","ports":"Transport Canada, Canada Port Authorities, 2024 (preliminary)","airports":"Statistics Canada, 2025 passengers enplaned and cargo"},"sources":["natural_earth","tc_ports","statcan_airports","ourairports"],"road_classes":[{"id":"national","name":"National highway"},{"id":"major","name":"Major highway"}],"rail_tiers":[{"id":"main","name":"Main line"},{"id":"secondary","name":"Secondary line"}],"rules":{"roads":"Natural Earth 1:10m roads in Canada at scale rank 8 or less, sampled every 4 km and snapped to hex-centre links. Class 0: scale rank 5 or less (the Trans-Canada, Yellowhead and other national routes and the main freeways); class 1: scale rank 6 to 8. Natural Earth's Interstate tag is not used: it marks Quebec's autoroutes but not Ontario's 400-series highways.","rail":"Natural Earth 1:10m railroads, sampled every 4 km and snapped to hex-centre links; tier 0 (main line) is scale rank 5 or less, as in the U.S. edition. Lower-ranked lines stay as secondary lines (the U.S. edition drops ranks above 7) because Canada's sparse network depends on remote lines.","ports":"The 17 Canada Port Authorities by cargo tonnage in 2024 (preliminary), in tonnes (Transport Canada table M17). Each stands at Natural Earth's port point (Thunder Bay and Belledune at their official place names). Private and provincial ports are not included.","airports":"Every airport with 10,000+ passengers enplaned in 2025, and every airport handling 10,000+ tonnes of cargo (Statistics Canada tables 23-10-0253 and 23-10-0254), placed by ICAO code with OurAirports. Hub classes apply the FAA's shares of national boardings (large 1%+, medium 0.25%+, small 0.05%+). Statistics Canada suppresses some airports for confidentiality, among them Whitehorse, Iqaluit, Charlottetown, Kingston and Kitchener/Waterloo, so they are not shown. Left out with no land hex within 2 rings, as in the U.S.: Îles-de-la-M